Agreed. My earlier #2 was too loosely stated.

A plain tube does not become a useful pneumatic resonator merely because it is attached to an oscillating source. For an open or closed air column, the fundamental frequency is roughly

\[
f\sim \frac{c}{4L}
\quad\text{or}\quad
f\sim \frac{c}{2L},
\]

with \(c\approx343\ \mathrm{m/s}\). So even a 1 m tube has an acoustic fundamental on the order of 86–172 Hz. To resonate at a diaphragm pump’s few-hertz pulsation rate would require tubing tens of metres long, unless a large compliant chamber and a narrow restriction form a low-frequency Helmholtz-type system.

More importantly, in your reversed ejector setup the oscillation was generated while two continuous jets opposed one another inside the throat region. That makes a **self-excited jet-interaction mode** the much more plausible primary mechanism:

- one jet displaces or suppresses the other;
- pressure accumulates behind the restricted inlet nozzle;
- the suppressed jet breaks through;
- the impingement region shifts;
- pressure is released;
- the cycle repeats.

The tubing and attached volumes could still modify the frequency or amplitude, but they would be a **load or feedback impedance**, not the fundamental cause of oscillation.

A better formulation would have been:

> The remaining question is whether the observed waveform was governed entirely by the internal colliding-jet instability, or whether the external pneumatic load altered or locked that instability.

That distinction is plausible. An independently generated jet oscillator can synchronize with downstream compliance without the tubing itself being capable of originating the oscillation.

What you describe is plausible, but I would distinguish **bubble formation**, **boiling**, and **net vapor removal**. The liquid may still be vaporizing readily while the apparatus becomes very poor at actually removing that vapor from the liquid inventory.

A likely regime is:

\[
\text{liquid}
\rightleftharpoons
\text{microbubble population}
\rightleftharpoons
\text{vapor space}
\]

rather than the simpler one-way process:

\[
\text{liquid}\rightarrow\text{escaping vapor}.
\]

The oscillatory pressure field can repeatedly grow, compress, collapse, and regenerate bubbles. Heat is consumed when liquid enters the bubbles and returned locally when some vapor condenses, but the vapor is not necessarily exported from the system. The result can look like enormous heat consumption with surprisingly little net loss of liquid.

Several mechanisms could produce the behavior you observed.

### Persistent microbubble–liquid state

The bubbles may contain a mixture of:

- vapor of the working liquid;
- previously dissolved air or other noncondensable gas;
- decomposition gases, once overheating begins.

A purely vapor-filled bubble can disappear completely when pressure rises or temperature falls. A bubble containing even a small noncondensable-gas core is much harder to eliminate. Vapor condenses around that core, but the gas nucleus survives and expands again during the next low-pressure excursion.

That creates a continuously renewed microbubble population rather than complete vaporization.

### Large apparent increase in vaporization heat

The intrinsic latent heat has not necessarily increased. Instead, the **energy cost per unit of vapor actually removed** can increase immensely because energy is being spent on:

- repeated evaporation and condensation;
- bubble expansion and compression;
- recirculating vapor back into the liquid;
- heating liquid that remains trapped in a foam or bubbly dispersion;
- heat loss through the enlarged apparatus and prolonged operating time.

Thus:

\[
\frac{\text{heat input}}{\text{net liquid removed}}
\]

can become very large, even though the ordinary latent heat of vaporization is unchanged.

It is somewhat analogous to operating a distillation pot with extremely strong internal reflux but poor product takeoff.

### Why the liquid can appear resistant to boiling

Microbubbles ordinarily provide nucleation sites, so they should not make initial boiling more difficult. But they can interfere with **bubble growth, coalescence, and disengagement**.

If the liquid becomes densely populated by small bubbles:

- small bubbles may remain entrained rather than rising out;
- oscillatory shear may continually fragment larger bubbles;
- a stable froth can develop;
- gas holdup can reduce liquid circulation near the heater;
- a bubbly or foamy layer can separate the bulk liquid from the vapor outlet;
- vapor can repeatedly recondense before escaping.

The system may therefore exhibit vigorous microscale phase change without the familiar macroscopic behavior of freely rising and escaping boiling bubbles.

A useful term would be **vapor disengagement limitation** rather than literal suppression of vaporization.

### Why overheating and degradation become easier

This follows naturally if vapor production no longer provides an effective temperature clamp.

During ordinary open boiling, generated vapor escapes and carries latent heat away. The liquid temperature stays near its boiling temperature at the prevailing pressure. In the regime you describe, however:

- vapor may be retained or condensed back;
- bubble fragmentation may prevent efficient buoyant removal;
- the heater surface may become partly insulated by gas;
- local liquid circulation may deteriorate;
- temperature measurements may understate heater-surface temperature.

The bulk liquid can therefore appear only mildly boiling while thin liquid films near the heat source reach much higher temperatures.

For organic materials, that promotes:

- pyrolysis;
- polymerization;
- oxidation;
- cracking;
- discoloration;
- viscosity increase;
- formation of additional gaseous products.

There is also a feedback loop:

\[
\text{thermal degradation}
\rightarrow
\text{noncondensable gases}
\rightarrow
\text{more persistent bubbles}
\rightarrow
\text{poorer vapor disengagement}
\rightarrow
\text{greater overheating}.
\]

If air was present anywhere in the oscillator loop, the microbubbles would also create enormous gas–liquid interfacial area and could accelerate oxidative degradation. Even without intentional air injection, dissolved oxygen released from the liquid could contribute initially.

So I would characterize your observation as a transition from **ordinary nucleate boiling with vapor escape** to an **oscillatory, gas-retaining two-phase dispersion with strong internal vapor recycling**. The apparent “resistance to boiling” is likely resistance to complete evaporation and vapor removal, not resistance to the molecular act of vaporization itself.
